            <h1 class="section-title">Palaeography Workshops</h1>
            <div class="parallel__block">
                <div class="parallel__block__item_1">
                    <p>The Scottish Records Association (SRA) offers palaeography classes to help
                    researchers read and understand handwritten records produced in Scotland in the
                    early modern period. The workshops are run on a voluntary basis by members of the
                    SRA who have relevant experience in reading early modern records and teaching
                    palaeography. The classes were originally designed to be offered on-site for small
                    groups, but they have been modified to be presented online as well.</p>
                    <h2>Palaeography Pointers</h2>
                    <p>This workshop lasts about an hour and is for people who are starting to read records
                    written in the seventeenth and early-eighteenth centuries. It uses examples from kirk
                    session records and parish registers of baptisms and marriages.</p>
                    <h2>Palaeography for Private Household Collections</h2>
                    <p>This workshop introduces researchers and archive staff to the skills and techniques
                    necessary to read and interpret Scottish household papers, in the early modern
                    period, and deals with correspondence, journals, and other household records from
                    the seventeenth century. The documents used in the course are from Glamis Castle
                    Archives, but they are record types found in most historic house collections. It is in
                    two parts, with each part lasting 2-3 hours. It can be run as two separate events or
                    as a whole-day event.</p>
                    <h2>Previous workshops</h2>
                    <p>The Palaeography Pointers workshop took place most recently in Aberdeen in
                    October 2024. The Palaeography for Private Household Collections took place in
                    Aberdeen in October 2024 and in the Muniments Room at Argyll Estates Archives at
                    Inveraray Castle in September 2025.</p>
                    <h2>Future workshops</h2>
                    <p>If you would like to host a palaeography
                    workshop, please get in touch with the Membership Secretary via the <a href="/contact/">Contact page</a>.</p>
